137	     * If set()/setValue() throws an Error, we let it propagate. Why? The most likely Error is a
138	     * StackOverflowError (from deep transform(..., directExecutor()) nesting), and calling
139	     * setException(stackOverflowError) would fail:
140	     *
141	     * - If the stack overflowed before set()/setValue() could even store the result in the output
142	     * Future, then a call setException() would likely also overflow.
143	     *
144	     * - If the stack overflowed after set()/setValue() stored its result, then a call to
